,"cnm_p089","cnm_121","cnm_116","cnm_055","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:93925f338a9a59d09ffa65f0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 1+sqrt(2)","reason":"The outer centers form a square of side 2r, so its center-to-vertex distance is r*sqrt(2). Thus r*sqrt(2)=r+1, giving r=1/(sqrt(2)-1)=1+sqrt(2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_121"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_10B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_121","cnm_058","cnm_042","cnm_171"],"id":"center-distances","label":"Join the circle centers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"center-distances":["cnm_h181","cnm_122","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h181","cnm_122","cnm_174","cnm_250","cnm_121","cnm_058","cnm_042","cnm_171"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:93925f338a9a59d09ffa65f0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 1/5","reason":"If the common height is h, the yard has area 25h and the trapezoid has area (15 + 25)h/2 = 20h. The flower-bed fraction is therefore (25h - 20h)/(25h) = 1/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_121"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_10B_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p077","cnm_110","cnm_121","cnm_p185"],"id":"triangular-bed-areas","label":"Find the triangle legs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangular-bed-areas":["cnm_p150","cnm_055","cnm_p148","cnm_p089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_055","cnm_p148","cnm_p089","cnm_p077","cnm_110","cnm_121","cnm_p185"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:93925f338a9a59d09ffa65f0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 12√3","reason":"Let each hexagon side be s. A perpendicular projection in a 30° ear triangle gives BF²=(2-√3)s². The total area is (√3/4)(2-√3)s²+3s²/4=(√3/2)s². Equating this to 6√3 gives s²=12, so the perimeter is 6s=12√3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_121"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_121","cnm_p148","cnm_173"],"id":"method_1","label":"Separate the three pointed triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"method_1":["cnm_p150","cnm_123","cnm_139","cnm_140","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_123","cnm_139","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_121","cnm_p148","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:93925f338a9a59d09ffa65f0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 3√35","reason":"Let BY=u and DX=v. Similarity gives v/u=3/2. Also AY=4 and CX=3, so AB=CD gives u²+16=v²+9. Hence u²=28/5, v²=63/5, and u+v=√35. Since AC=6, the area is 6(u+v)/2=3√35.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_121"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_121","cnm_055","cnm_064","cnm_p148","cnm_075"],"id":"method_1","label":"Use the equal diagonals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"method_1":["cnm_p150","cnm_174","cnm_123"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p150","cnm_174","cnm_123","cnm_121","cnm_055","cnm_064","cnm_p148","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:93925f338a9a59d09ffa65f0"],"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Coordinator checked explicit alternate solutions against displayed English and revised the prior conventional-reading disposition.","derivedAnswer":"C under a crossing-arcs convention; the radius CD/2 also produces a meeting point and valid bisector.","reason":"The displayed English says arcs meeting at P inside the angle and asks for intersection, without requiring a crossing or two circle intersections. Let theta be the nonstraight angle and OC=OD=R. The chord midpoint M is inside the angle, CM=DM=CD/2, and OM bisects the angle by congruent right triangles. Equal-radius circles centered at C,D are tangent at M when r=CD/2; their arcs meet there. Thus strictly greater than CD/2 is not necessary for the stated construction. The narrower Chinese technical distinction between crossing and tangency was not supplied to the learner. Preserve the intended C key but do not enforce exclusive grading.","recordedAnswer":"C","status":"confirmed_ambiguous"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_121"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["circle_intersection_means_crossing_not_tangency","The construction uses crossing arcs with two intersection points; a tangent contact at radius CD/2 is not the source technical meaning of intersecting circles.","confirmed_answer_ambiguity"],"questionId":"cnm_127_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_p188","cnm_120"],"id":"radius-cases-and-midpoint","label":"Distinguish crossing from tangent meeting","methodDirectNodeIds":{"radius-cases-and-midpoint":["cnm_127","cnm_111","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_058"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_127","cnm_111","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_058","cnm_055","cnm_p188","cnm_120"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:93925f338a9a59d09ffa65f0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: d = a + b","reason":"Number consecutive vertices V0 through V8 and let c be the chord spanning three sides. In cyclic quadrilateral V0V1V3V6, the sides are a, b, c, c and the diagonals are c, d. Ptolemy gives cd = ac + bc, so d = a + b.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_121"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_180","cnm_121"],"id":"equilateral-cut-and-congruence","label":"Split the longest diagonal using an equilateral triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equilateral-cut-and-congruence":["cnm_266","cnm_252","cnm_253","cnm_139","cnm_123","cnm_055"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_266","cnm_252","cnm_253","cnm_139","cnm_123","cnm_055","cnm_250","cnm_180","cnm_121"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:93925f338a9a59d09ffa65f0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 4.0","reason":"The perpendicular projections of A and B leave equal overhangs of (10-4)/2=3. Thus B's foot is 7 units from D. Since B is the midpoint of DE, its horizontal coordinate is half that of E, giving DF=14 and CF=14-10=4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_121"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1989_AMC_12_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p077","cnm_p105","cnm_064","cnm_067","cnm_110"],"id":"perpendicular-feet-and-midpoint","label":"Use perpendicular feet and the midpoint condition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"perpendicular-feet-and-midpoint":["cnm_055","cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_185","cnm_p077","cnm_p105","cnm_064","cnm_067","cnm_110"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:93925f338a9a59d09ffa65f0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 1679","reason":"Let O be the midpoint of AB and T the contact point on AD. The horizontal offset of AD is (92-19)/2=73/2, so AT=46·(73/2)/x=1679/x. The condition AT≤x gives x²≥1679, with equality when T=D.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_121"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1992_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_132","cnm_260"],"id":"solution:similar-right-triangles:c1a78cba5293","label":"Locate the tangency points","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similar-right-triangles":["cnm_262","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_174","cnm_261"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_262","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_174","cnm_261","cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_132","cnm_260"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:93925f338a9a59d09ffa65f0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 2 - √3","reason":"The legs satisfy a+b=√3 and a²+b²=2. Therefore (b-a)²=2(a²+b²)-(a+b)²=1. Since b>a, b-a=1, giving a/b=(√3-1)/(√3+1)=2-√3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_121"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_10A_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p077","cnm_p078","cnm_p089","cnm_121","cnm_074"],"id":"pythagorean-sum-and-difference","label":"Find the sum and difference of the legs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"pythagorean-sum-and-difference":["cnm_p185","cnm_174","cnm_148","cnm_171"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p185","cnm_174","cnm_148","cnm_171","cnm_p077","cnm_p078","cnm_p089","cnm_121","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"alignmentIds":["alignment:93925f338a9a59d09ffa65f0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 2√3-3 square inches","reason":"The square has side 1. HL congruence of triangles DMC and BNC gives AM=AN=t. Equilateral side equality gives 2t²=1+(1-t)², so t=√3-1. Subtracting the three corner right triangles from the square gives area 1-[t²+2(1-t)]/2=2√3-3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_121"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1974_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_121","cnm_p089","cnm_173"],"id":"equal-sides-and-area-subtraction","label":"Equal sides and area subtraction","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-sides-and-area-subtraction":["cnm_139","cnm_p148","cnm_125","cnm_174","cnm_221","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_p148","cnm_125","cnm_174","cnm_221","cnm_p150","cnm_191","cnm_121","cnm_p089","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"alignmentIds":["alignment:93925f338a9a59d09ffa65f0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 75°","reason":"Set B=(0,0), D=(1,0), and C=(3,0). Since ∠ABC=45°, write A=(t,t). The 60° direction of DA gives t=sqrt(3)(t-1), so t=(3+sqrt(3))/2. Therefore tan(∠ACB)=t/(3-t)=2+sqrt(3)=tan(75°). The angle is acute, hence it is 75°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_121"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2001_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_062","cnm_121"],"id":"equilateral-and-isosceles-construction","label":"Create matching isosceles triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equilateral-and-isosceles-construction":["cnm_130","cnm_139","cnm_123","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_130","cnm_139","cnm_123","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_115","cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_062","cnm_121"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":11,"alignmentIds":["alignment:93925f338a9a59d09ffa65f0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, DF need not bisect ∠CDA","reason":"Write ∠A=α. The diameter gives ∠CDA=90°. Tangent geometry yields FC=FD=FA, ∠FDA=α, ∠CFD=2α, and ∠BCD=α. Thus F is the midpoint of CA and the other stated relations hold, but DF bisects ∠CDA only when α=45°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_121"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_116","cnm_250","cnm_260"],"id":"right-triangle-congruence-and-angles","label":"Compare two right triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"right-triangle-congruence-and-angles":["cnm_254","cnm_262","cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_117"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_254","cnm_262","cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_117","cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_116","cnm_250","cnm_260"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_113","cnm_116","cnm_250","cnm_260"],"id":"construct-hypotenuse-midpoint","label":"Construct the midpoint of CA","methodDirectNodeIds":{"construct-hypotenuse-midpoint":["cnm_254","cnm_262","cnm_187","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_137","cnm_117"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_254","cnm_262","cnm_187","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_137","cnm